  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThis Afternoon - Evening: Additional Heavy Rain Potential in \nSouthwest Wisconsin and Northeast Iowa\n\nHeavy rain impacts shift this afternoon to portions of northeast \nIowa and southwest Wisconsin with additional convective development \npossible as shown in several of the CAMs. The general conceptual \nmodel as we head later into the day is for the front responsible for \nmuch of our rainfall to sag south with some surface convergence \nalong it. As this occurs, some instability attempts to be advected \ninto the region as southerly low-level flow pushes the warm sector \nto our south northward. The key question will be if this instability \ncan be advected northward enough in light of the convective debris \nover the region to support robust convection and if the forcing will \nbe sufficient. Many of the CAMs show some degree of convection \nduring the afternoon/evening but disagree on how organize it would \nbe. There are some pretty notable differences between various CAMs \nsuch as the HRRR which has shown some tendency to convect all the \nway up to I-90 as opposed to solutions like the ARW which keeps \nconvection limited across the local area. Consequently, confidence \nremains somewhat low as to how impactful any additional storms may \nbe but given that soils are very susceptible given several locations \nseeing upwards of 5-8\" of rain last night. \n\nAs far as additional rainfall amounts are concerned, overall the \n25th to 75th percentile spread in the recent HREF would suggest more \nwidespread totals across portions of northeast Iowa and southwest \nWisconsin of around 0.5\" to 2\". However, given the 90th percentile \nvalues in the HREF showing values of 2-4\" would think underneath \nheavier storms that this could be feasible, which in turn could \nexacerbate any area flooding on rivers and low-lying areas. To see \nadditional details on ongoing flooding concerns, refer to the Hydro \nsection for more information. Otherwise, cannot completely rule out \na weak tornado in far southern Grant or Clayton counties given \nfairly decent low-level hodograph curvature and some tendency to \nwetbulb the warm nose and allow for convection to be surface based. \nGiven the lack of deep-layer shear any updrafts will be short-lived \nbut something to consider for the mid to late afternoon \ntimeframe.\n\n\nUpcoming Week: Cool and Drier \n\nThe lower tropospheric baroclinic zone slides to the south of the \nregion by Sunday morning, taking the bulk of the rainfall risk with \nit. Cannot fully rule out some stray elevated showers scraping parts \nof east-central Iowa during the day, but most of the region will \nstay dry with some sun poking out as we move through the afternoon. \nThe mean longwave trough/cutoff low currently over the Inter-\nMountain West slides eastward for Monday night into Tuesday, \nbringing with it the next potential for wider coverage showers. \nHowever, with the deeper moisture displaced well to the south of the \nregion, any impacts from this rain will be low. Eastern flow off a \nGreat Lakes/Ontario high will keep temperatures seasonably cool and \nmostly dry for the remainder of the week.",
